Grouping words by first letter used to take an if-null-then-put dance. With Map.computeIfAbsent it's one line:

map.computeIfAbsent(word.charAt(0), k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(word);

If the key is missing, the lambda creates the list and stores it; either way you get the list back to add to. One caution: don't modify the same map from inside that lambda.

Since Java 7, anything that implements AutoCloseable can go in the brackets of try (...). Java closes it for you when the block ends — even if an exception is thrown — and closes several resources in reverse order of opening. If closing also fails, that second exception is attached as 'suppressed' instead of hiding the first.

try (var in = Files.newBufferedReader(path)) {
    return in.readLine();
}

Since Java 14 (JEP 361), switch can return a value, and arrow labels don't fall through:

int days = switch (month) {
  case FEB -> 28;
  case APR, JUN, SEP, NOV -> 30;
  default -> 31;
};

No break statements, and over an enum the compiler checks that every case is covered when you leave out default.

When you need to split a list into two groups by a yes/no test, partitioningBy is clearer than groupingBy:
Map<Boolean, List<Order>> split = orders.stream().collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(o -> o.total() > 1000));
Both keys, true and false, are always present, even if one list is empty, so you never get a surprise null.

Records can check their data without repeating every field:

record Range(int lo, int hi) {
    Range {
        if (lo > hi) throw new IllegalArgumentException("lo > hi");
    }
}

The compact constructor has no parameter list; the fields are assigned automatically after its body runs. You can even normalise values there by reassigning the parameters, like lo = Math.max(0, lo).

Counting words used to take an if/else. Now it's one line:

counts.merge(word, 1, Integer::sum);

If the key is absent, merge stores 1. If it's present, it combines the old and new values with Integer::sum. If the function returns null, the entry is removed.
